2026-10-28T02:19+00:00  🌊 Lake Erie running warm for Oct 26: 62°F, +4°F vs the 1995–2025 average (record for the date: 62.1°F in 2017, now 61.9°F). Extra lake-effect fuel.
2026-10-28T02:59+00:00  🌪️ The polar vortex may break down. The polar vortex is a huge spinning wind high above the Arctic that usually keeps the coldest air locked up north. In 20 of 31 runs of the American weather model (65%), that spin stops and reverses, as early as Nov 4. When that happens, Arctic air often spills south 1 to 3 weeks later, which can mean cold and snow for Buffalo. Yesterday it was 35%.
2026-10-28T02:59+00:00  🌊 Lake Erie is unusually warm for Oct 26: 61.9°F, about 4°F warmer than normal. The record for this date is 62.1°F (2017). Warm lake water is fuel for lake-effect snow: when cold air blows over it, the lake adds heat and moisture, which can build heavier snow bands.
2026-10-28T03:09+00:00  ❄️ Lake-effect snow setup. When cold air flows over the warmer lake, the lake heats and moistens it, and it rises into snow bands. Forecasters look for the lake to be about 23°F (13°C) warmer than the air 5,000 feet up. In 26 of 31 runs of the American weather model (84%), that happens over Lake Erie around Oct 31. Wind direction decides who gets buried.
2026-10-28T20:49+00:00  🗺️ NOAA's forecast for Nov 3–Nov 7 got more confident: Buffalo now has a 70% chance of being warmer than normal (before: 60% chance warmer than normal). This comes from NOAA's Climate Prediction Center, which forecasts whether the weeks ahead will run warmer or colder than usual.
